        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Every burning smell earns a stop, a switch-off and a
            cool-down.</strong> Hot plastic, acrid, fishy or ozone smells, smoke, sparking, or a
            smell with a tripped breaker mean do not run it again until it has been inspected.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Burning rubber is usually friction — a dry deck, a belt rubbing the frame, or a slipping
            drive belt — and is fixable with
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">lubrication</a>,
            tension and tracking. Fix it promptly, because a dry deck today is a
            <strong class="text-white">motor or controller board</strong> tomorrow.
          </p>
        </div>
